data.content:
Here are the two files:

**`SortableList.razor`** — markup with `@typeparam TItem`, a `<ul>` loop using `@key` for efficient diffing, and Move Up/Down buttons that are disabled at the boundaries.

**`SortableList.razor.cs`** — `partial class` code-behind with:

- **`Items`** (`IReadOnlyList<TItem>`) — copied to a private `List<TItem>` in `OnParametersSet`, so the parent's list is never mutated.
- **`ItemTemplate`** (`RenderFragment<TItem>`) — renders each item via `@context`.
- **`OnOrderChanged`** (`EventCallback<IReadOnlyList<TItem>>`) — fires after every swap, sending back a read-only snapshot of the reordered list.

Usage matches your example — the parent receives the updated list and owns the state:

```razor
<SortableList Items="@tasks" OnOrderChanged="HandleReorder">
    <ItemTemplate>
        <span>@context.Title</span>
    </ItemTemplate>
</SortableList>

@code {
    private List<TaskItem> tasks = new() { /* ... */ };

    private void HandleReorder(IReadOnlyList<TaskItem> updated)
    {
        tasks = updated.ToList();
    }
}
```
